d they would provide a statement to  global news but did not return a request  for comment by deadline about where the  money was coming from and there's no  question the canadian armed forces need  money in an increasingly dangerous world  general air warns the global order may  be at a turning point more fragile than  it's been in generations we've seen  signs of that unraveling russia's  invasion of ukraine is now in its fourth  month meanwhile chinese fighter jets  have been aggressively buzzing canadian  and australian aircraft in international  airspace air says canadians should brace  for more confrontation between liberal  democracies like canada on one hand and  authoritarian regimes on the other over  the course of the last number of weeks  i've i've traveled  and have had  numerous conversations with my my  counterparts chiefs of defense in in our  closest allies in europe um in asia  pacific and they are all very concerned  the threat of uh global conflict of  great power conflict as is is as great  as it has been in decades uh so it uh we  need to be worried we'll have more on  that and how the military is trying to  deal with sexual misconduct ahead on the  show tomorrow farah  all right mercedes stevenson in ottawa  thank you mercedes and you can watch  mercedes full interview with canada's  chief of the defense staff tomorrow on  the west block right here on global  ahead another pandemic pitfall why  lifeguards are in short supply this  summer season  watching global national  kids looking to cool off this summer at  the beach or pool may face  disappointment as the country grapples  with a major lifeguard shortage swim  instructors have also become hard to  come by and it is all a byproduct of the  pandemic and as heather york's west  explains with pools open again and kids  eager to learn swimming skills  municipalities are scrambling to cope  after a long cold winter brook duvall is  looking forward to spending a summer  with her kids in the water but making  sure her children have the swimming  skills they need has not been easy  through the pandemic was tough because  there were  lessons started and then cancelled  and then for the most part there weren't  lessons at all then when the duvals  finally found lessons this spring  unfortunately about a week before the  lessons were supposed to happen it was  canceled because they didn't have  lifeguards the city of toronto says it's  had to cancel about 15 percent of its  summer swim programs because of staffing  in calgary that number is 55 percent but  the problem extends nationwide it really  stemmed from the  the pandemic two years ago when  everything shut down in march of 2020  many lifeguards and swim instructors  were furloughed or lost their jobs  and not everyone has come back the  shutdown has also meant the next  generation of lifeguards who would  usually be preparing for the role in  their early teens haven't been able to  access the necessary courses  across the country communities have had  to reduce pool hours and swim programs i  think it's going to take time for us to  get back to our pre-pandemic service  level you know over the next year we're  going to work diligently at it  the tight labor market has meant many  municipalities have had to get creative  west vancouver found success recruiting  uncertified candidates early in the  season the selected candidates were then  offered the opportunity to have free  training and we would put them through  their nl their national lifeguard and  their wsi which is their water safety  instructor we are now predicting that  we'll be fully staffed by the middle of  july  back in toronto duvall is hopeful for  better luck finding lessons this fall  planning to teach her boys some skills  herself over the summer as they spend as  much time as they can at the pool  heather urex west global news calgary  next what if it didn't cost a lot to get  to space a canadian startup competing  with spacex to send 